<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 682–720</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Umar II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, known as Umar II, was the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 717 to 720. Remembered as the most pious of the dynasty, he sought to reform taxation so that non-Arab converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>, were treated as equals of Arab Muslims, ended the cursing of Ali from the pulpit and withdrew the army from the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His short reign became the model of a just ruler in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz.jpg" alt="Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Umar_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Umar was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. His father, Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan, belonged to the ruling Umayyad family, and his mother, Layla bint Asim, was a granddaughter of the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> caliph Umar. Historians later stressed this descent to set his character apart from other Umayyad caliphs. His father governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 685, and Umar spent part of his childhood there before being educated in Medina, where he formed ties with pious men and transmitters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">Al-Walid I</a> appointed him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 706, with his jurisdiction later extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a>. Traditional accounts call him a just governor. From 707 he rebuilt and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> became a refuge for Iraqi exiles fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a>, who pressured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> to dismiss Umar in 712. He stayed at court and became a principal adviser to Sulayman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the traditional sources, Sulayman on his deathbed at Dabiq was persuaded by Raja ibn Haywa to name Umar as his successor, though Reinhard Eisener thinks Raja's role overstated. The choice passed over the descendants of Abd al-Malik, and Umar averted conflict by naming Yazid II as his own successor. He acceded on 22 September 717 and overhauled the provincial administrations, appointing governors for their reliability and integrity.</p><p>His most significant reform was the equality of Arab and non-Arab Muslims. Converts no longer paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, but their land became the property of their villages and stayed liable to the full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a>, a safeguard against collapse of revenue. He restricted the appropriation of crown lands as private estates and is credited with ordering the first official collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>. Because of the cost of campaigns and the losses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he withdrew the army from the siege, though Muslim forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> conquered and fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> in his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Umar fell ill on a journey and died between 5 and 10 February 720 at Dayr Sim'an near Maarat al-Numan, where he was buried in a plot he had bought with his own funds. Yazid II succeeded him. Muslim tradition is unanimous, in Hugh Kennedy's words, that he was pious and ruled like a true Muslim, unlike the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. He was widely regarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and many Muslims count him the fifth rightly guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> spared his body from the desecration they applied to other Umayyad caliphs. Kennedy calls him the most puzzling of the Marwanid rulers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The pious caliph <b>Umar II</b> (r. 717–720) attempted to relieve converts of these burdens, but the problem persisted to the end of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Church of Saint Simeon Stylites, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=706" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5745 5441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5745 5441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=706">What was here in 706 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
